APX.de = {"country":{"name":"Germany","tagline":"Slate, sugar and the northern edge of ripeness: the country that taught the world what Riesling can do, now making some of Europe's most exciting Pinot Noir.","classification":"Two pyramids now coexist on German labels, and a wine nerd needs both.\n\nThe legal pyramid starts with Deutscher Wein (no origin beyond Germany) and Landwein (a PGI, 26 regions). Most wine is Qualitätswein or Prädikatswein, which are PDO wines from one of the 13 Anbaugebiete. Under the 2021 reform, origin is layered inside the PDO: the Anbaugebiet (a wine labelled only 'Pfalz' is meant to be dry), the Region (the old Bereich or Großlage, which must now be printed as 'Region Michelsberg' and the like), the Ortswein from a single village (85 percent of grapes from the village), and the Lagenwein from a single vineyard, which must name both village and site ('Hochheimer Hölle'). A Gewann, a lieu-dit within a vineyard, may appear if it is registered. The rule of thumb written into the law is: the smaller the origin, the stricter the rules.\n\nThe Prädikat ladder survives as a measure of grape ripeness at harvest: Kabinett, Spätlese, Auslese, Beerenauslese (BA), Trockenbeerenauslese (TBA) and Eiswein. Prädikatswein may not be chaptalised. In practice Kabinett, Spätlese and Auslese increasingly signal fruity sweetness, while dry wines are labelled trocken; 'feinherb' is an unregulated term for off-dry.\n\nThe private pyramid belongs to the VDP: Gutswein (estate wine), Ortswein (village), Erste Lage (premier cru) and Grosse Lage (grand cru). Dry wine from a Grosse Lage is a Grosses Gewächs (GG), hand-picked, capped at 50 hl/ha and released no earlier than September of the year after harvest (reds a year later). Since the 2024 vintage the terms Grosses Gewächs and Erstes Gewächs are also defined in the national wine ordinance, and responsibility for classifying vineyards as Erste or Grosse Lage has shifted to the VDP and the national growers' association, so the two pyramids are slowly converging.\n\nSparkling wine is Sekt; Sekt b.A. comes from a named Anbaugebiet, and Winzersekt is grower-made by the traditional method. Liebfraumilch is a legally defined, mild Qualitätswein from Rheinhessen, Pfalz, Rheingau or Nahe."},"regions":[{"name":"Mosel","slug":"mosel","tagline":"Slate cliffs, river loops and Riesling of impossible lightness.","ids":["de-bernkastel","de-burg-cochem","de-mosel","de-moseltor","de-obermosel","de-ruwertal","de-saar","de-uhlen-blaufusser-lay","de-uhlen-laubach","de-uhlen-roth-lay"]},{"name":"Rheingau","slug":"rheingau","tagline":"A single south-facing slope that became the cradle of fine Riesling.","ids":["de-johannisberg","de-rheingau"]},{"name":"Nahe","slug":"nahe","tagline":"Germany's geological patchwork, where volcano meets slate.","ids":["de-monzinger-niederberg","de-nahe","de-nahetal"]},{"name":"Rheinhessen","slug":"rheinhessen","tagline":"Germany's largest region, reinvented by a generation of young growers.","ids":["de-bingen","de-nierstein","de-rheinhessen","de-wonnegau"]},{"name":"Pfalz","slug":"pfalz","tagline":"Germany's Tuscany: warm, generous, and home to the 'three Bs' of Riesling.","ids":["de-mittelhaardt-deutsche-weinstrasse","de-pfalz","de-sudliche-weinstrasse"]},{"name":"Baden","slug":"baden","tagline":"Germany's warm south, from Lake Constance to Tauberfranken, ruled by Pinot.","ids":["de-baden","de-badische-bergstrasse","de-bodensee","de-breisgau","de-kaiserstuhl","de-kraichgau","de-markgraflerland","de-ortenau","de-tauberfranken","de-tuniberg"]},{"name":"Franken","slug":"franken","tagline":"Silvaner in a flattened flask, from the shell limestone of the Main.","ids":["de-burgstadter-berg","de-franken","de-wurzburger-stein-berg"]},{"name":"Württemberg","slug":"wurttemberg","tagline":"Red-wine country on the Neckar, where Stuttgart has vineyards inside the city.","ids":["de-wurttemberg"]},{"name":"Ahr","slug":"ahr","tagline":"A narrow slate canyon making Pinot Noir at the 50th parallel, rebuilt after the 2021 flood.","ids":["de-ahr"]},{"name":"Mittelrhein","slug":"mittelrhein","tagline":"Castles, the Loreley and steep slate Riesling in a UNESCO gorge.","ids":["de-mittelrhein"]},{"name":"Hessische Bergstraße","slug":"hessische-bergstra-e","tagline":"Germany's springtime garden on the edge of the Odenwald.","ids":["de-hessische-bergstrasse"]},{"name":"Saale-Unstrut","slug":"saale-unstrut","tagline":"Germany's northernmost classic region, on shell-limestone terraces.","ids":["de-saale-unstrut"]},{"name":"Sachsen","slug":"sachsen","tagline":"Baroque palaces and granite terraces on the Elbe around Dresden.","ids":["de-sachsen"]}],"items":{"de-ahr":{"name":"Ahr","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Ahr","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973)","area_ha":531,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Spätburgunder, Frühburgunder, Riesling","summary":"The Ahr PDO is a narrow slate gorge of about 530 hectares in seven municipalities, and one of the most northerly places in the world for serious Pinot Noir. Spätburgunder covers about two thirds of the vineyard and Frühburgunder is a speciality. After the catastrophic flood of July 2021 the growers rebuilt cellars and wineries; production in 2025 rose sharply from the depressed post-flood years.","terroir":"Devonian slate and greywacke on steep terraces that store heat in a sheltered valley.","producers":["Meyer-Näkel","Jean Stodden","Deutzerhof","Kreuzberg","J.J. Adeneuer","Nelles","Winzergenossenschaft Mayschoß-Altenahr"],"nerd_note":"Meyer-Näkel's sisters Meike and Dörte Näkel clung to a tree for hours during the 2021 flood before being rescued, then rebuilt and released the 2021 vintage.","rslug":"ahr"},"de-baden":{"name":"Baden","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Baden","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973)","area_ha":15679,"styles":["white","red","rose","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Spätburgunder, Müller-Thurgau, Grauburgunder, Weißburgunder, Riesling, Gutedel","summary":"Baden's PDO covers about 15,700 hectares in roughly 400 municipalities, stretched across nine Bereiche from Tauberfranken to Lake Constance. As the only German region in EU wine zone B, it has slightly different rules on alcohol and enrichment, and it is the home of German Pinot: Spätburgunder is the leading variety and the Burgundian family covers well over half the vineyard. The volcanic Kaiserstuhl, the granite Ortenau and the limestone Breisgau and Markgräflerland each have a distinct style.","terroir":"Warm Upper Rhine plain between Black Forest and Vosges; granite, volcanic tuff, loess, limestone and moraine.","producers":["Bernhard Huber","Dr. Heger","Salwey","Franz Keller","Bercher","Ziereisen","Fritz Wassmer","Andreas Laible","Schloss Neuweier","Enderle und Moll","Martin Wassmer"],"nerd_note":"Baden's 'Weißherbst' is a rosé made from a single red variety and stated as such on the label.","contains":["Kaiserstuhl","Tuniberg","Ortenau","Breisgau","Markgräflerland","Bodensee","Kraichgau","Badische Bergstraße","Tauberfranken"],"rslug":"baden"},"de-badische-bergstrasse":{"name":"Badische Bergstraße","cat":"Bereich","region":"Baden","subregion":"Badische Bergstraße","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"summary":"The Baden continuation of the Bergstraße south of Heidelberg, around Weinheim, Schriesheim and Wiesloch, with Riesling and Pinot on granite and loess at the foot of the Odenwald; it shares the early spring and almond blossom of its Hessian neighbour.","terroir":"Granite and loess.","rslug":"baden"},"de-bernkastel":{"name":"Bernkastel","cat":"Bereich","region":"Mosel","subregion":"Middle Mosel","created":"1971","styles":["white","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling","summary":"Bereich Bernkastel is the Middle Mosel from Zell to Trier-area villages, the heartland of the region's fame: Piesport, Brauneberg, Bernkastel, Graach, Wehlen, Zeltingen, Ürzig and Erden. A wine labelled only with the Bereich name is a blend from anywhere in that stretch; the excitement lies in the single villages and vineyards inside it.","terroir":"Blue slate in the main river bends, red slate at Ürzig and Erden.","producers":["Dr. Loosen","Joh. Jos. Prüm","Selbach-Oster","Markus Molitor","Fritz Haag","Wwe. Dr. H. Thanisch"],"nerd_note":"The famous Großlage Bernkasteler Kurfürstlay sits inside this Bereich and must now be labelled 'Region Kurfürstlay'.","rslug":"mosel"},"de-bingen":{"name":"Bingen","cat":"Bereich","region":"Rheinhessen","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Riesling, Silvaner, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"The north-western Bereich of Rheinhessen around Bingen, Ingelheim and the Rheinhessische Schweiz. It includes Bingen's quartzite Scharlachberg, Ingelheim's red wine tradition and the porphyry soils of Siefersheim, where Wagner-Stempel works.","terroir":"Quartzite, porphyry, loess and limestone.","producers":["Wagner-Stempel","Neus","Bettina Schumann"],"nerd_note":"Ingelheim calls itself the 'Rotweinstadt', a red-wine tradition dating to Charlemagne's palace.","rslug":"rheinhessen"},"de-bodensee":{"name":"Bodensee","cat":"Bereich","region":"Baden","subregion":"Bodensee","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"summary":"Baden's Lake Constance vineyards around Meersburg and Hagnau, at around 400 to 500 metres, benefit from the lake's warmth. Müller-Thurgau and Spätburgunder dominate, including Weißherbst rosé.","terroir":"Glacial moraine and molasse.","producers":["Staatsweingut Meersburg","Aufricht"],"nerd_note":"The Meersburg state winery traces its roots to the Prince-Bishops of Konstanz.","rslug":"baden"},"de-breisgau":{"name":"Breisgau","cat":"Bereich","region":"Baden","subregion":"Breisgau","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"summary":"The Breisgau north of Freiburg is limestone and loess country whose star is Malterdingen, where Bernhard Huber (died 2014; now Julian Huber) showed that Muschelkalk can give Pinot Noir of Burgundian finesse. Cistercians from Tennenbach are said to have brought Pinot here.","terroir":"Shell limestone, Keuper and loess.","producers":["Bernhard Huber","Enderle und Moll"],"rslug":"baden"},"de-burg-cochem":{"name":"Burg Cochem","cat":"Bereich","region":"Mosel","subregion":"Lower Mosel","created":"1971","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Riesling, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"Burg Cochem is the Lower Mosel or Terrassenmosel, from Zell to Koblenz, with Bremm's Calmont, Cochem's castle and the terraced cliffs of Winningen and Kobern-Gondorf. Growers like Heymann-Löwenstein and Knebel have shown that its terraces give powerful, dry Rieslings.","terroir":"Hard slate, quartzite and sandstone on dry-stone terraces.","producers":["Heymann-Löwenstein","Knebel","Lubentiushof","Franzen"],"nerd_note":"The Uhlen at Winningen was split into three separately registered EU PDOs in 2018 according to its soils.","rslug":"mosel"},"de-burgstadter-berg":{"name":"Bürgstadter Berg","cat":"PDO","region":"Franken","subregion":"Churfranken","created":"2017","styles":["red","white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Spätburgunder, Frühburgunder and others","summary":"An EU PDO registered in 2017 for the red sandstone hillside of Bürgstadt on the lower Main, the first German single-site PDO. Bürgstadt is the home of Rudolf Fürst, whose Centgrafenberg and Hundsrück Spätburgunder are among Germany's greatest reds.","terroir":"Buntsandstein (red sandstone) on steep south-facing slopes over the Main.","producers":["Rudolf Fürst","Weingut Helmut Fürst"],"nerd_note":"The Centgrafenberg is named after the medieval 'Centgraf', a district judge.","rslug":"franken"},"de-franken":{"name":"Franken","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Franken","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973)","area_ha":6173,"styles":["white","red","sparkling","sweet"],"grapes":"Silvaner, Müller-Thurgau, Bacchus, Riesling, Domina, Spätburgunder","summary":"The Franken PDO covers about 6,200 hectares in 141 municipalities along the Main, mostly in Lower Franconia. It is the home of dry Silvaner and the protected Bocksbeutel bottle. The Würzburger Stein, Randersackerer Pfülben, Escherndorfer Lump, Iphöfer Julius-Echter-Berg and Rödelseer Küchenmeister make the region's great whites, while the red sandstone of Bürgstadt and Klingenberg gives some of Germany's finest Spätburgunder from Rudolf Fürst and Benedikt Baltes.","terroir":"Triassic layers of red sandstone, shell limestone and Keuper; continental climate with frost risk.","producers":["Juliusspital","Bürgerspital","Staatlicher Hofkeller Würzburg","Rudolf Fürst","Hans Wirsching","Horst Sauer","Zehnthof Luckert","Rudolf May","Benedikt Baltes","Fürstlich Castell'sches Domänenamt","Schmitt's Kinder"],"nerd_note":"Franken growers still use the term 'Frankenwein ist Männerwein'; the region's dry tradition is old enough that many wines never bothered with the word trocken.","contains":["Würzburger Stein-Berg","Bürgstadter Berg"],"rslug":"franken"},"de-hessische-bergstrasse":{"name":"Hessische Bergstraße","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Hessische Bergstraße","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973)","area_ha":461,"styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Riesling, Grauburgunder, Spätburgunder, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"One of Germany's smallest regions at about 460 hectares in ten municipalities between Darmstadt and Heppenheim. Riesling and Pinot Gris grow on granite and loess along the Odenwald, in a climate so mild that spring blossom arrives weeks before the rest of Germany.","terroir":"Granite and loess on the western edge of the Odenwald.","producers":["Hessische Staatsweingüter Domäne Bergstraße","Simon-Bürkle","Bergsträsser Winzer"],"nerd_note":"The state estate's Heppenheimer Centgericht is a monopole named after a medieval court.","rslug":"hessische-bergstra-e"},"de-johannisberg":{"name":"Johannisberg","cat":"Bereich","region":"Rheingau","created":"1971","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Riesling","summary":"Bereich Johannisberg is the single district covering the whole Rheingau. A wine with only this name is a regional blend; it should not be confused with Schloss Johannisberg or the village of Johannisberg, both far more specific.","nerd_note":"Wines labelled 'Bereich Johannisberg' must from the 2026 vintage show the word 'Region' in front of the name.","rslug":"rheingau"},"de-kaiserstuhl":{"name":"Kaiserstuhl","cat":"Bereich","region":"Baden","subregion":"Kaiserstuhl","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"summary":"The Kaiserstuhl is an extinct volcano about 15 km across that rises from the Rhine plain north-west of Freiburg, and its Bereich is the warmest part of Germany. Terraces of volcanic tuff and deep loess at Ihringen, Achkarren, Oberrotweil, Burkheim and Oberbergen give powerful Spätburgunder, Grauburgunder and Weißburgunder; Ihringen regularly records Germany's highest temperatures.","terroir":"Volcanic tephrite and tuff overlain in places by loess up to tens of metres deep.","producers":["Dr. Heger","Salwey","Franz Keller","Bercher","Freiherr von Gleichenstein","Winzergenossenschaft Achkarren"],"nerd_note":"Loess terraces were bulldozed into huge flat platforms in the 1970s land consolidation, a scar still visible from the air.","rslug":"baden"},"de-kraichgau":{"name":"Kraichgau","cat":"Bereich","region":"Baden","subregion":"Kraichgau","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"summary":"A hilly loess and Keuper landscape between Heidelberg and Heilbronn, the 'land of a thousand hills', with mixed plantings of Riesling, Pinot and Müller-Thurgau, much of it handled by cooperatives, and a few ambitious estates in villages such as Sulzfeld and Eichtersheim.","terroir":"Loess, Keuper marl, shell limestone.","rslug":"baden"},"de-markgraflerland":{"name":"Markgräflerland","cat":"Bereich","region":"Baden","subregion":"Markgräflerland","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"summary":"Between Freiburg and Basel, the Markgräflerland is the home of Gutedel (Chasselas), introduced in 1780 by Margrave Karl Friedrich from Vevey. It also makes fine Pinot and Chardonnay on limestone and loess, with stars such as Ziereisen and the Wassmer brothers.","terroir":"Loess and limestone, warm and sunny below the Black Forest.","producers":["Ziereisen","Fritz Wassmer","Martin Wassmer","Hanspeter Ziereisen"],"nerd_note":"Gutedel is the same grape as Switzerland's Chasselas and covers about a third of the Markgräflerland vineyard.","rslug":"baden"},"de-mittelhaardt-deutsche-weinstrasse":{"name":"Mittelhaardt-Deutsche Weinstraße","cat":"Bereich","region":"Pfalz","subregion":"Mittelhaardt","created":"1971","styles":["white","red","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling, Spätburgunder","summary":"The northern Bereich of the Pfalz from Bockenheim to Neustadt, containing the classic Riesling villages of Kallstadt, Ungstein, Bad Dürkheim, Wachenheim, Forst, Deidesheim and Ruppertsberg. This is where the 'three Bs' and Christmann farm their grand crus.","terroir":"Sandstone, limestone, loess and the basalt of Forst.","producers":["Bürklin-Wolf","Bassermann-Jordan","Reichsrat von Buhl","Christmann","Koehler-Ruprecht"],"rslug":"pfalz"},"de-mittelrhein":{"name":"Mittelrhein","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Mittelrhein","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973)","area_ha":460,"styles":["white","sparkling","red"],"grapes":"Riesling, Spätburgunder, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"The Mittelrhein PDO covers about 460 hectares in 51 municipalities in the Rhine gorge from Bingen to near Bonn, mostly on steep slate. Riesling dominates, giving crisp, mineral wines. Bacharach, Steeg, Oberwesel and Boppard are the key villages, and much of the area lies in the Upper Middle Rhine Valley World Heritage Site.","terroir":"Slate and greywacke terraces above the Rhine.","producers":["Toni Jost","Ratzenberger","Matthias Müller","Florian Weingart","Lanius-Knab","Didinger"],"nerd_note":"Bacharach takes its name, according to tradition, from Bacchus' altar (Bacchi ara), a rock in the river.","rslug":"mittelrhein"},"de-monzinger-niederberg":{"name":"Monzinger Niederberg","cat":"PDO","region":"Nahe","created":"2018","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Riesling","summary":"A small single-site PDO at Monzingen on the upper Nahe, registered by the EU in 2018 for Riesling with a maximum yield of 75 hl/ha. It is one of the handful of German vineyards to seek protection at EU level outside the Anbaugebiet system, a sign of growers wanting to anchor quality to a precise place.","terroir":"Slate and weathered volcanic rock on a slope near Monzingen.","nerd_note":"Monzingen is better known for the Halenberg and Frühlingsplätzchen, Emrich-Schönleber's two Grosse Lagen.","rslug":"nahe"},"de-mosel":{"name":"Mosel","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Mosel","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973; renamed from Mosel-Saar-Ruwer in 2007)","area_ha":8287,"styles":["white","sweet","sparkling","red"],"grapes":"Riesling (about 62%), Müller-Thurgau, Elbling, Weißburgunder, Spätburgunder, Kerner","summary":"The Mosel PDO covers the river from the Luxembourg border to Koblenz plus the Saar and Ruwer valleys: about 8,300 hectares (2025) in 131 municipalities, most of them on slopes too steep for machines. It is the benchmark for Riesling at low alcohol and high acidity. A Mosel Kabinett might have 8 percent alcohol and 40 grams of sugar yet taste dry-ish and electric; a Spätlese is richer, an Auslese honeyed, and the region's TBA and Eiswein are among the most expensive sweet wines in the world. Dry Riesling, often labelled trocken or Grosses Gewächs, is now just as important, and grower Sekt from Riesling or Elbling is rising.\n\nThe best sites cluster in the Middle Mosel between Trittenheim and Ürzig, at Bernkastel, Graach, Wehlen and Zeltingen, and on the Saar around Wiltingen and Ockfen. Abandoned terraces on the Lower Mosel and side valleys are being replanted by young growers, a sign of a region in revival.","terroir":"Devonian slate in blue, grey and red forms, steep south-facing slopes over a river that reflects light and buffers frost.","producers":["Egon Müller","Joh. Jos. Prüm","Dr. Loosen","Markus Molitor","Fritz Haag","Zilliken","Clemens Busch","Heymann-Löwenstein","Van Volxem","Selbach-Oster","Willi Schaefer","Reichsgraf von Kesselstatt","Peter Lauer","Maximin Grünhaus","Karthäuserhof"],"nerd_note":"The Mosel's traditional gold capsule (Goldkapsel) and long gold capsule mark an estate's best Auslese, a private code that predates the 1971 law.","contains":["Saar","Ruwertal","Bernkastel","Burg Cochem","Obermosel","Moseltor","Uhlen Blaufüsser Lay / Uhlen Blaufüßer Lay","Uhlen Laubach","Uhlen Roth Lay"],"rslug":"mosel"},"de-moseltor":{"name":"Moseltor","cat":"Bereich","region":"Mosel","subregion":"Upper Mosel (Saarland)","created":"1971","styles":["white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Elbling, Auxerrois, Pinot varieties","summary":"Moseltor is the small Saarland end of the Mosel around Perl and Nennig, opposite Schengen in Luxembourg, where three countries meet. Limestone soils and Pinot varieties link it to Luxembourg and Lorraine.","terroir":"Muschelkalk limestone.","nerd_note":"A Roman villa at Nennig preserves one of the largest mosaics north of the Alps, a reminder that wine and wealth arrived here early.","rslug":"mosel"},"de-nahe":{"name":"Nahe","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Nahe","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973)","area_ha":4250,"styles":["white","red","sweet","sparkling"],"grapes":"Riesling, Müller-Thurgau, Dornfelder, Grauburgunder, Weißburgunder","summary":"The Nahe PDO covers about 4,250 hectares in 91 municipalities along the Nahe and its side valleys, from Monzingen to Bingen. It is Germany's most geologically varied region, and its best Rieslings combine the Mosel's lift with the Rheingau's body. The middle Nahe holds the stars: Dönnhoff's Hermannshöhle and Brücke, the Kupfergrube and Felsenberg at Schlossböckelheim, Emrich-Schönleber's Halenberg and Frühlingsplätzchen at Monzingen, and Schäfer-Fröhlich's Felseneck at Bockenau.","terroir":"Slate, quartzite, porphyry, melaphyre, sandstone and loess in a sunny, dry valley.","producers":["Dönnhoff","Emrich-Schönleber","Schäfer-Fröhlich","Kruger-Rumpf","Schlossgut Diel","Gut Hermannsberg","Tesch","Hexamer"],"nerd_note":"The Nahe was split from the Rheinhessen and Mosel orbit only in 1971; before that its wines were often sold under neighbours' names.","contains":["Nahetal","Monzinger Niederberg"],"rslug":"nahe"},"de-nahetal":{"name":"Nahetal","cat":"Bereich","region":"Nahe","created":"After 1971 (merger of Bereiche Kreuznach and Schloss Böckelheim)","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Riesling and others","summary":"The single Bereich covering the whole Nahe, created when the two older Bereiche (Kreuznach and Schloss Böckelheim) were merged. A wine labelled only Nahetal is a regional blend; from the 2026 vintage it must be printed as 'Region Nahetal', and the region's real interest lies in its villages and single sites.","rslug":"nahe"},"de-nierstein":{"name":"Nierstein","cat":"Bereich","region":"Rheinhessen","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Riesling, Silvaner, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"The eastern Bereich along the Rhine from Mainz to Worms-north, including Nackenheim, Nierstein and Oppenheim. Its jewel is the Roter Hang, a cliff of red Permian slate and sandstone above the river. The infamous Großlage Niersteiner Gutes Domtal also sits here.","terroir":"Red Rotliegend slate and sandstone on the river cliff; loess inland.","producers":["Kühling-Gillot","Gunderloch","St. Antony","Schätzel","Heyl zu Herrnsheim"],"nerd_note":"Oppenheim's Deutsches Weinbaumuseum documents the region's long history.","rslug":"rheinhessen"},"de-obermosel":{"name":"Obermosel","cat":"Bereich","region":"Mosel","subregion":"Upper Mosel","created":"1971","styles":["white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Elbling, Auxerrois, Weißburgunder, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"The Upper Mosel faces Luxembourg across the river south of Trier and sits on Muschelkalk limestone, not slate. It is the last stronghold of Elbling, one of Europe's oldest varieties, which gives light, tart wines and good Sekt, alongside the Pinot family.","terroir":"Limestone and marl, the same geology as Luxembourg's Moselle.","producers":["Weingut Hild","Margarethenhof"],"nerd_note":"Elbling is thought to have been grown on the Mosel since Roman times.","rslug":"mosel"},"de-ortenau":{"name":"Ortenau","cat":"Bereich","region":"Baden","subregion":"Ortenau","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"summary":"The Ortenau between Baden-Baden and Offenburg grows Riesling (locally 'Klingelberger') and Spätburgunder on steep granite slopes at Durbach, Neuweier and Oberkirch. Neuweier is one of the few places outside Franken allowed to use the Bocksbeutel.","terroir":"Weathered granite and gneiss of the Black Forest.","producers":["Andreas Laible","Schloss Neuweier","Markgraf von Baden (Schloss Staufenberg)","Heinrich Männle"],"nerd_note":"In Durbach Riesling is called Klingelberger after a vineyard at Schloss Staufenberg.","rslug":"baden"},"de-pfalz":{"name":"Pfalz","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Pfalz","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973; named Rheinpfalz until the 1990s)","area_ha":23793,"styles":["white","red","rose","sweet","sparkling"],"grapes":"Riesling, Dornfelder, Müller-Thurgau, Spätburgunder, Grauburgunder, Weißburgunder","summary":"The Pfalz PDO runs along the Haardt from Bockenheim to Schweigen with about 23,800 hectares in 142 municipalities. It is warm, dry and diverse, making the country's fullest dry Rieslings, fine Pinot, and good everyday reds. The Mittelhaardt's Forst, Deidesheim, Wachenheim, Ungstein and Kallstadt hold the greatest Riesling sites; the Südliche Weinstraße around Birkweiler, Siebeldingen and Schweigen excels with Pinot and Riesling on red sandstone and limestone.","terroir":"Sheltered by the Haardt forest; sandstone, limestone, loess and basalt.","producers":["Bürklin-Wolf","Bassermann-Jordan","Reichsrat von Buhl","Ökonomierat Rebholz","Dr. Wehrheim","Friedrich Becker","Christmann","Koehler-Ruprecht","Philipp Kuhn","Knipser","Mosbacher","Kranz","Pfeffingen"],"nerd_note":"The region was officially called Rheinpfalz until the 1990s; Bavarian rule (1816 to 1945) left the 1828 land-tax classification that still informs site rankings.","contains":["Mittelhaardt-Deutsche Weinstraße","Südliche Weinstraße"],"rslug":"pfalz"},"de-rheingau":{"name":"Rheingau","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Rheingau","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973)","area_ha":3207,"styles":["white","red","sweet","sparkling"],"grapes":"Riesling (about 76%), Spätburgunder","summary":"The Rheingau PDO covers about 3,200 hectares in eleven municipalities from Flörsheim and Hochheim on the Main to Lorch at the start of the Rhine gorge. It is the most Riesling-dominated region in Germany and the historic home of German fine wine: the place of Kloster Eberbach, Schloss Johannisberg, Schloss Vollrads and the Geisenheim institute. Rheingau Riesling is broader than Mosel Riesling, with peach and citrus fruit, spice and a firm, dry backbone, and the region led the movement back to dry Riesling with the Charta (1983) and its Erstes Gewächs (1999). Spätburgunder from the slate slopes of Assmannshausen is a celebrated red.\n\nThe great sites run along the slope: Rüdesheimer Berg Schlossberg and Rottland, Johannisberger Hölle, Winkeler Jesuitengarten, Erbacher Marcobrunn and Siegelsberg, the Steinberg, Kiedricher Gräfenberg, Rauenthaler Baiken and Hochheimer Hölle and Domdechaney.","terroir":"A south-facing slope sheltered by the Taunus with the wide Rhine as a mirror; loess and marl near the river, slate and quartzite higher up.","producers":["Robert Weil","Georg Breuer","Peter Jakob Kühn","Künstler","Leitz","Schloss Johannisberg","Schloss Vollrads","Kloster Eberbach","Spreitzer","August Kesseler","Eva Fricke","Weingut Chat Sauvage"],"nerd_note":"The word 'hock' for Rhine wine comes from Hochheim; after Queen Victoria visited in 1845 a vineyard there was renamed Königin-Victoriaberg.","contains":["Johannisberg"],"rslug":"rheingau"},"de-rheinhessen":{"name":"Rheinhessen","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Rheinhessen","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973)","area_ha":27499,"styles":["white","red","rose","sweet","sparkling"],"grapes":"Müller-Thurgau, Riesling, Dornfelder, Silvaner, Grauburgunder, Spätburgunder","summary":"Germany's largest PDO at about 27,500 hectares in 133 municipalities, Rheinhessen produced over two million hectolitres in 2025 and makes everything from bulk wine to some of the world's most expensive dry Riesling. Its best sites are on the Roter Hang at Nierstein and Nackenheim, the limestone of the Wonnegau around Westhofen and Dalsheim, Bingen's Scharlachberg and the Rheinhessische Schweiz around Siefersheim. Silvaner, Weißburgunder and Spätburgunder are all excellent here.","terroir":"A warm, dry plateau; loess and marl over limestone, red Rotliegend slate at the Roter Hang.","producers":["Keller","Wittmann","Kühling-Gillot","Gunderloch","Schätzel","Battenfeld-Spanier","Wagner-Stempel","St. Antony","Dreissigacker","Groebe"],"nerd_note":"Keller's G-Max, from undisclosed old vines in the Wonnegau, has fetched thousands of euros per bottle at auction.","contains":["Bingen","Nierstein","Wonnegau"],"rslug":"rheinhessen"},"de-ruwertal":{"name":"Ruwertal","cat":"Bereich","region":"Mosel","subregion":"Ruwer","created":"1971","styles":["white","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling","summary":"The Ruwertal Bereich covers the little Ruwer valley east of Trier, with Kasel, Waldrach, Mertesdorf and Eitelsbach. The Rieslings are delicate and herbal, with very low alcohol and long ageing potential. Two monastic estates, Maximin Grünhaus and the Karthäuserhof, own their slopes as monopoles.","terroir":"Blue Devonian slate on steep south-facing slopes in a cool valley.","producers":["Maximin Grünhaus","Karthäuserhof","Erben von Beulwitz","Reichsgraf von Kesselstatt"],"nerd_note":"Karthäuserhof's bottles carry only a neck label and no body label, a tradition dating to the nineteenth century.","rslug":"mosel"},"de-saale-unstrut":{"name":"Saale-Unstrut","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Saale-Unstrut","created":"1990 (after reunification; EU reg. 1996)","area_ha":853,"styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Müller-Thurgau, Weißburgunder, Silvaner, Riesling, Bacchus","summary":"The Saale-Unstrut PDO covers about 850 hectares in 51 municipalities in Saxony-Anhalt, Thuringia and Brandenburg, around 51 degrees north. Dry whites from shell-limestone terraces dominate, and Freyburg is home to Rotkäppchen-Mumm, Germany's largest sparkling wine company.","terroir":"Muschelkalk and sandstone terraces in a dry continental climate.","producers":["Pawis","Lützkendorf","Landesweingut Kloster Pforta","Klaus Böhme","Hey","Rotkäppchen"],"nerd_note":"The Werder area near Potsdam in Brandenburg belongs to Saale-Unstrut for legal purposes despite lying 150 km away.","rslug":"saale-unstrut"},"de-saar":{"name":"Saar","cat":"Bereich","region":"Mosel","subregion":"Saar","created":"1971","styles":["white","sweet"],"grapes":"Riesling","summary":"The Saar Bereich covers the lower Saar valley from Serrig to Konz, with the villages of Saarburg, Ockfen, Ayl, Wiltingen, Kanzem, Wawern and Oberemmel. It is cooler and later-ripening than the Middle Mosel, and its Rieslings have the highest acidity and some of the most precise flavours in Germany. Scharzhofberg is the most famous site; Bockstein, Rausch, Kupp and Altenberg are close behind.","terroir":"Grey slate and diabase on steep, wind-exposed slopes.","producers":["Egon Müller","Zilliken","Peter Lauer","Van Volxem","von Othegraven","Dr. Fischer","Hofgut Falkenstein"],"nerd_note":"Peter Lauer labels its wines by 'Fass' numbers that correspond to historic parcels within the Ayler Kupp.","rslug":"mosel"},"de-sachsen":{"name":"Sachsen","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Sachsen","created":"1990 (after reunification; EU reg. 1996)","area_ha":522,"styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Müller-Thurgau, Riesling, Weißburgunder, Grauburgunder, Traminer, Goldriesling","summary":"Sachsen's PDO covers about 520 hectares along the Elbe from Pirna to Diesbar-Seußlitz in 55 municipalities, the easternmost region in Germany. Granite terraces, baroque estates and dry, aromatic whites define it; Goldriesling is a local curiosity.","terroir":"Granite, syenite and gneiss on south-facing Elbe terraces.","producers":["Schloss Proschwitz","Klaus Zimmerling","Martin Schwarz","Schloss Wackerbarth","Vincenz Richter","Karl Friedrich Aust"],"nerd_note":"Goldriesling, a 1893 Alsatian crossing, survives almost only in Saxony.","rslug":"sachsen"},"de-sudliche-weinstrasse":{"name":"Südliche Weinstraße","cat":"Bereich","region":"Pfalz","subregion":"Südpfalz","created":"1971","styles":["white","red","sparkling"],"grapes":"Riesling, Pinot varieties, Müller-Thurgau","summary":"The southern Bereich from Neustadt to the French border. Long a cooperative stronghold, it has become a source of exceptional Pinot Noir, Weißburgunder and Riesling from estates in Birkweiler, Siebeldingen, Leinsweiler, Ilbesheim and Schweigen, some of whose vineyards lie across the border in Alsace.","terroir":"Red sandstone, Rotliegend, limestone and loess.","producers":["Ökonomierat Rebholz","Dr. Wehrheim","Friedrich Becker","Kranz","Siegrist","Münzberg"],"nerd_note":"Friedrich Becker's best Pinot Noir comes from vineyards in Wissembourg, France, which German law lets him label as Pfalz.","rslug":"pfalz"},"de-tauberfranken":{"name":"Tauberfranken","cat":"Bereich","region":"Baden","subregion":"Tauberfranken","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"summary":"Baden's northernmost Bereich in the Tauber valley around Tauberbischofsheim and Lauda, culturally Franconian, with Muschelkalk slopes, a continental climate, Bocksbeutel bottles and the old red Tauberschwarz grape, which almost vanished before local growers revived it.","terroir":"Shell limestone.","nerd_note":"Tauberschwarz, nearly extinct by the 1950s, was revived here.","rslug":"baden"},"de-tuniberg":{"name":"Tuniberg","cat":"Bereich","region":"Baden","subregion":"Tuniberg","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"summary":"A small limestone ridge west of Freiburg, next to the Kaiserstuhl, planted mostly with Spätburgunder and Pinot whites and dominated by cooperatives.","terroir":"Jurassic limestone under loess.","rslug":"baden"},"de-uhlen-blaufusser-lay":{"name":"Uhlen Blaufüsser Lay / Uhlen Blaufüßer Lay","cat":"PDO","region":"Mosel","subregion":"Lower Mosel","created":"2018","styles":["white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Riesling","summary":"One of three EU PDOs carved from the Winninger Uhlen on the Lower Mosel in 2018, at the initiative of Reinhard Löwenstein, who argued that the amphitheatre-shaped Uhlen contains three distinct soils. Blaufüßer Lay is the blue-grey slate section with calcareous inclusions; its Riesling is the most fine-boned and saline of the three. Rules require Riesling, a maximum yield of 70 hl/ha and hand harvesting.","terroir":"Blue-grey Devonian slate with lime-rich fossil layers on terraced cliffs above the Mosel.","producers":["Heymann-Löwenstein","Knebel"],"nerd_note":"Among the first single-vineyard PDOs in Germany; the name means 'blue-foot slate'.","rslug":"mosel"},"de-uhlen-laubach":{"name":"Uhlen Laubach","cat":"PDO","region":"Mosel","subregion":"Lower Mosel","created":"2018","styles":["white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Riesling","summary":"The Laubach section of the Winninger Uhlen, registered as an EU PDO in 2018, lies on grey slate rich in fossils. Its Rieslings are broad and smoky, often with more body than Blaufüßer Lay. Like its siblings it requires Riesling, hand harvest and low yields.","terroir":"Grey slate with fossil-bearing layers.","producers":["Heymann-Löwenstein"],"nerd_note":"Löwenstein's three Uhlen bottlings side by side are a textbook lesson in soil and taste.","rslug":"mosel"},"de-uhlen-roth-lay":{"name":"Uhlen Roth Lay","cat":"PDO","region":"Mosel","subregion":"Lower Mosel","created":"2018","styles":["white","sparkling"],"grapes":"Riesling","summary":"Roth Lay, the reddish, iron-rich slate section of the Uhlen straddling Winningen and Kobern-Gondorf, became an EU PDO in 2018. It gives the most powerful, spicy wines of the Uhlen trio.","terroir":"Red, iron-bearing slate.","producers":["Heymann-Löwenstein"],"nerd_note":"The only one of the three Uhlen PDOs whose area extends into Kobern-Gondorf.","rslug":"mosel"},"de-wonnegau":{"name":"Wonnegau","cat":"Bereich","region":"Rheinhessen","created":"1971","styles":["white","red"],"grapes":"Riesling, Silvaner, Spätburgunder","summary":"The southern Bereich around Worms, Westhofen, Flörsheim-Dalsheim and Monsheim, whose limestone hills have become the epicentre of modern Rheinhessen. Keller and Wittmann made Morstein, Kirchspiel, Brunnenhäuschen and Hubacker famous.","terroir":"Limestone, marl and loess.","producers":["Keller","Wittmann","Battenfeld-Spanier","Dreissigacker","Groebe"],"nerd_note":"The Liebfrauenstift vineyards around Worms' Liebfrauenkirche lie in this Bereich.","rslug":"rheinhessen"},"de-wurttemberg":{"name":"Württemberg","cat":"Anbaugebiet","region":"Württemberg","created":"1971 (EU reg. 1973)","area_ha":11392,"styles":["red","white","rose","sparkling"],"grapes":"Trollinger, Riesling, Lemberger, Schwarzriesling, Spätburgunder","summary":"Württemberg's PDO covers about 11,400 hectares in 375 municipalities around the Neckar and Stuttgart, and is Germany's red-wine region par excellence, with red grapes on two thirds of the land. Light Trollinger and Schwarzriesling are local staples; Lemberger and Spätburgunder from ambitious estates now rival the best in Germany. The region also covers the Bavarian shore of Lake Constance around Lindau.","terroir":"Steep Neckar slopes of Muschelkalk and Keuper marl; warm summers.","producers":["Aldinger","Rainer Schnaitmann","Dautel","Graf Neipperg","Karl Haidle","Wöhrwag","Jürgen Ellwanger","Fürst Hohenlohe-Oehringen","Herzog von Württemberg","Beurer"],"nerd_note":"Württemberg Schillerwein is a protected Rotling: red and white grapes pressed and fermented together.","rslug":"wurttemberg"},"de-wurzburger-stein-berg":{"name":"Würzburger Stein-Berg","cat":"PDO","region":"Franken","subregion":"Maindreieck","created":"2020","styles":["white"],"grapes":"Silvaner, Weißburgunder, Riesling","summary":"Registered by the EU in 2020, this single-site PDO covers the Würzburger Stein and adjacent Steinberg slopes above the city. The rules are strict: Silvaner, Riesling or Weißburgunder only, 50 hl/ha maximum and dry wines. It turns the most famous vineyard of Franken into a legally protected origin of its own, a model of how German growers are using EU law to fix quality to place.","terroir":"Shell limestone (Muschelkalk) on a south-facing slope above the Main, with the warmth of the city.","producers":["Juliusspital","Bürgerspital","Staatlicher Hofkeller Würzburg"],"nerd_note":"Würzburg's Steinwein gave the name 'Stein' to Franconian wine in general in the eighteenth and nineteenth centuries.","rslug":"franken"}}};
